[38;5;252m[1m## What the project turned out to be[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc_src[0m is a single-file cute_c2-derived 2D collision library (38 exported functions) with one public entry point, [38;5;10mcapsule()[0m. [38;5;10mCargo.toml[0m has [1mno [38;5;10m[features][0m section[22m, so there is exactly one build configuration — I confirmed this and verified both [38;5;10mcargo check[0m and [38;5;10mcargo check --no-default-features[0m build clean, rather than inventing combinations.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## The one real bug found[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Both libraries agreed on every finite input immediately. The divergence was in [1mNaN payload propagation[22m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc2Dot((NaN⁺,NaN⁺),(NaN⁺,NaN⁻))  C = 0xffc00000   Rust = 0x7fc00000
c2Mulvs((NaN⁺,NaN⁺), NaN⁻)      C = (NaN⁺,NaN⁺)  Rust = (NaN⁻,NaN⁻)
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
When both operands of an x86 SSE arithmetic instruction are NaN, the hardware returns whichever one sits in the destination register. Which source-level operand lands there is a register-allocation decision, and GCC at [38;5;10m-O0[0m (the documented build: no [38;5;10mCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE[0m) makes a different choice than LLVM for several expressions. I read the reference disassembly to map the [38;5;10msrc1[0m operand for every arithmetic site — e.g. [38;5;10mc2Dot[0m emits [38;5;10maddss %xmm1,%xmm0[0m where [38;5;10m%xmm0[0m holds the [3msecond[23m product.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Simply reordering the Rust operands does not fix this: LLVM canonicalises the operand order of commutative float ops on its own, which is exactly why [38;5;10mc2Mulvs[0m's [38;5;10ma.x *= b[0m ended up preferring [38;5;10mb[0m. So I added a small [38;5;10msse[0m module ([38;5;10maddss[0m/[38;5;10msubss[0m/[38;5;10mmulss[0m/[38;5;10mdivss[0m/[38;5;10msqrtss[0m) that resolves the NaN cases explicitly [3mbefore[23m doing arithmetic. Once NaN is handled, [38;5;10m+[0m and [38;5;10m*[0m are exactly commutative, so the result no longer depends on codegen — confirmed by running the full suite against both the debug and the optimized [38;5;10m--release[0m cdylib.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two other things worth knowing:[0m[0m
- This payload behaviour is a property of the reference [3mbuild[23m, not the C source — at [38;5;10m-O2[0m GCC matches naive Rust. I pinned it to the documented [38;5;10m-O0[0m build and said so in the module comment.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mc2GJK[0m appends a simplex vertex without initialising [38;5;10mu[0m; if the [38;5;10miter < 20[0m cap were ever exhausted, C would read that indeterminate value. A probe test measures the real maximum (**4**) and asserts it stays under 20, so the suite fails loudly if that unverifiable path ever becomes reachable.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Test suite ([38;5;10mtranslation/tests/[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Both [38;5;10m.so[0m files are loaded via [38;5;10mlibloading[0m and every call — including the Rust side — goes through [38;5;10mdlsym[0m, so the [38;5;10m#[no_mangle][0m wrappers and the struct-by-value ABI are exercised. Built bottom-up: [38;5;10mlevel0[0m vector leaves, [38;5;10mlevel1[0m pointer/aggregate functions, [38;5;10mlevel2[0m GJK plus predicates and [38;5;10mcapsule()[0m, [38;5;10mlevel3[0m degenerate/non-finite geometry, [38;5;10mlevel4[0m NaN payloads, and [38;5;10mmeta_exports[0m for [38;5;10mnm -D[0m parity.[0m[0m
